WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.520
Every amino acid story has three acts—structure, charge, and behavior.

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.880
This roadmap shows how the shape of an amino acid's R group determines its charge at

3
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.880
physiological pH, and how that charge, in turn, governs what the amino acid

4
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.660
does—folding, binding, catalysis.

5
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.520
Keep this sequence in mind—R group first, charge second, function third.

6
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:31.553
At physiological pH, amino acids don't carry a net charge.

7
00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:37.153
They exist as zwitterions, simultaneously positive at the amino group and negative

8
00:00:37.153 --> 00:00:38.613
at the carboxyl group.

9
00:00:39.153 --> 00:00:44.593
Drop the pH and the carboxylate gets protonated; raise it and the amino group loses

10
00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:49.993
its proton. The zwitterion form dominates right in between, and that crossover point

11
00:00:49.993 --> 00:00:52.453
is the isoelectric point, or pI.

12
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.457
Let's pin down the rule you'll use for the rest of biochemistry.

13
00:00:57.137 --> 00:00:59.377
It's a seesaw between pH and pKa.

14
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:04.157
When the pH is greater than the pKa, the group gives up its proton.

15
00:01:04.577 --> 00:01:05.737
It's deprotonated.

16
00:01:06.097 --> 00:01:09.537
When the pH is less than the pKa, the group holds on.

17
00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:12.277
It's protonated. That's the whole rule.

18
00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:15.377
pH above pKa, proton off.

19
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:18.517
pH below pKa, proton on.

20
00:01:19.177 --> 00:01:24.177
Drill it until it's automatic, because every titration curve, every buffer, and

21
00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:28.817
every charged side chain you meet from here on comes back to this one comparison.

22
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.790
Titration is a three-step experiment.

23
00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:37.510
You add acid or base from a burette, monitor the pH in real time, and plot the

24
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:41.790
resulting curve. What the curve reveals is remarkably informative.

25
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:48.250
The flat, buffered regions identify the pKa values of every ionizable group, and the

26
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.750
midpoint between them marks the isoelectric point, where the net charge on the

27
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.390
molecule is exactly zero.

28
00:01:55.383 --> 00:02:00.663
When an amino acid has an ionizable sidechain, finding its isoelectric point follows

29
00:02:00.663 --> 00:02:01.663
a simple shortcut.

30
00:02:02.403 --> 00:02:04.103
First, draw a charge ladder.

31
00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:09.263
Listing all protonation states from most positive at the top to most negative at the

32
00:02:09.263 --> 00:02:12.783
bottom, locate the net zero zwitterionic form.

33
00:02:13.243 --> 00:02:17.863
The pI is simply the average of the two pKa values that bracket that neutral

34
00:02:17.863 --> 00:02:23.343
species. Find the neutral form first, then average the pKa values on either side.

35
00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:30.287
Histidine is the only standard amino acid with a pKa near physiological pH, roughly

36
00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:36.847
6.0. At pH 7.4, it sits just above its pKa and can rapidly toggle between

37
00:02:36.847 --> 00:02:38.607
protonated and neutral forms.

38
00:02:39.227 --> 00:02:44.147
That flip-flop ability makes histidine indispensable in enzyme active sites, metal

39
00:02:44.147 --> 00:02:45.987
coordination, and pH sensing.

40
00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:51.567
Think of it as a molecular switch biology keeps perpetually half-cocked, ready to

41
00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:53.787
donate or accept a proton on demand.
